<Lecture
  chapterSlug="04-functions"
  lessonSlug="04-tip-calculator"
  hook="Four functions. Three small helpers and one orchestrator. The grade is for decomposition as much as correctness."
>
  <h2>The four functions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>compute_tip(subtotal, tip_pct)</code> — tip amount</li>
    <li><code>compute_total(subtotal, tip_pct)</code> — subtotal + tip</li>
    <li><code>split_evenly(amount, people)</code> — per-person share</li>
    <li><code>bill_summary(subtotal, tip_pct, people)</code> — dict with all three</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout tone="warning" title="🔒 no_paste mode is ON">
    <p>Graded homework. Type it. Tutor maxes at L2.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The right shape</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="See how each helper does ONE thing"
    code={`def compute_tip(subtotal, tip_pct):
    return subtotal * tip_pct / 100

def compute_total(subtotal, tip_pct):
    return subtotal + compute_tip(subtotal, tip_pct)    # composition

def split_evenly(amount, people):
    return amount / people

def bill_summary(subtotal, tip_pct, people):
    tip = compute_tip(subtotal, tip_pct)
    total = compute_total(subtotal, tip_pct)
    per_person = split_evenly(total, people)
    return {
        "tip": round(tip, 2),
        "total": round(total, 2),
        "per_person": round(per_person, 2),
    }

print(bill_summary(50.00, 20, 4))
print(bill_summary(125.50, 18, 3))`}
  />

  <h2>Why FOUR functions instead of one?</h2>
  <p>
    You could write everything in one <code>bill_summary</code>. It would work.
    But three things would suffer:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Tip formula is computed multiple times</strong> — change it once, find it three places.</li>
    <li><strong>Each line does too much</strong> — hard to test pieces in isolation.</li>
    <li><strong>Names disappear</strong> — readers re-parse the arithmetic instead of trusting <code>compute_tip</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Acceptance criteria</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All 7 tests pass</li>
    <li><code>compute_total</code> CALLS <code>compute_tip</code></li>
    <li><code>bill_summary</code> CALLS at least 2 of the 3 helpers</li>
    <li>Dict values rounded to 2 decimals</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Bottom-up — the textbook's advice">
    <p>
      From Think Python §3.11: <em>"Dividing a long program into functions
      allows you to debug the parts one at a time and then assemble them
      into a working whole."</em> Write and test <code>compute_tip</code>
      first. Then <code>compute_total</code>. Then the others.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
